From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 16:25:12 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: enable PCIe on the FRITZ!Box 7560

All three root complexes are populated on this board: RC0 and RC1 carry
the two WiFi radios, RC2 the VRX318 DSL companion. Enable the three
ports and the three PHYs, and give them the board policy the SoC dtsi
deliberately leaves out.

The reset lines are gpio0 9, 8 and 16, active low -- the PERST# inputs
of the three slots. Bank, pin and polarity are hardware truth rather
than a reading of the vendor tree: the 4.9 kernel drives exactly these
on this device and enumerates an endpoint behind every port. The three
pads were left unclaimed when the LEDs and buttons landed, precisely so
this change could take them; update that note accordingly.

Inbound byte swapping is enabled on the two radio ports and disabled on
RC2. The SoC is big endian and the DWC core is not, so inbound DMA gets
swapped on the way through -- except for the VRX318, whose driver
already presents its shared memory the way the host wants it, and a
second swap would undo the first. The vendor tree reaches the same
split from the other direction: grx_common.dtsi turns inbound-swap on
for all three ports, and vrx318.dtsi, included only by boards carrying
that chip, turns it back off on RC2. Outbound is never swapped.

Spread spectrum clocking is on, on all three PHYs, which is what this
hardware has always run with: the vendor board file enables it and the
vendor PHY driver reads exactly the spelling the board file uses. We
ask for the same behaviour, spelling the property the way a mainline
driver reads it. (The property that goes unread in the vendor tree is
its SoC-level one, not the board override.)

Two vendor properties are not carried over. intel,rst-interval is a
name nothing reads -- mainline calls the same knob reset-assert-ms and
already defaults it to the 100 ms the vendor asks for -- and intel,iatu
is moot because the driver suppresses iATU programming structurally,
through its own child config accessors, rather than by a DT flag.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi
index 66a861434fc9..8d29b03f2b5a 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi
@@ -186,3 +186,52 @@ dect-reset-hog {
 		line-name = "dect-reset";
 	};
 };
+
+&pcie_phy0 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
+};
+
+&pcie_phy1 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
+};
+
+&pcie_phy2 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
+};
+
+/*
+ * Inbound byte swapping is enabled on the two radio ports and disabled
+ * on RC2. This SoC is big endian and the DWC core is not, so inbound
+ * DMA is byte-swapped on the way through -- except for the VRX318,
+ * whose driver already presents its shared memory in the endianness the
+ * host expects, and swapping again would undo it. The vendor tree
+ * arrives at the same split: grx_common.dtsi sets inbound-swap on all
+ * three, and vrx318.dtsi, included only by boards carrying that chip,
+ * clears it on RC2.
+ *
+ * Outbound is never swapped, on any port.
+ */
+&pcie0 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,inbound-swap = <1>;
+	intel,outbound-swap = <0>;
+	reset-gpios = <&gpio0 9 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+};
+
+&pcie1 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,inbound-swap = <1>;
+	intel,outbound-swap = <0>;
+	reset-gpios = <&gpio0 8 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+};
+
+/* VRX318 DSL companion -- see the inbound-swap note above. */
+&pcie2 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,inbound-swap = <0>;
+	intel,outbound-swap = <0>;
+	reset-gpios = <&gpio0 16 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+};
